{% comment %}
  Apply for wholesale (Figma: 508:13439, drawn there as "Apply for a wholesale account").

  The form is not this section's own. It is the Clay B2B Wholesale app's
  registration block, added as an `@app` block — so which fields there are, their
  order, what is required and what happens on submit all belong to the app's
  admin. That is what buys the two things Shopify's contact form could not give:
  the certificate is a real upload, and a submission creates a tagged customer
  rather than an email in a mailbox.

  What stays here is the page. The column, the heading and the copy above it, and
  the skin below, which puts the app's markup in the kit's clothes. Clay's own
  rules are single-class, so two classes here outweigh them and nothing needs
  `!important`; its fields are in the light DOM, so they can be reached at all.

  Clay also owns the state after sending — it swaps the form for its own message
  in place, with no redirect, which is why the `form.posted_successfully?` branch
  this section used to carry has gone with the form. It announces the send on
  `document` as `clay:form:submitted`, carrying the new customer. The section's
  own confirmation panel — the same tinted panel the corporate quote form shows,
  with the `sent-heading` and `sent-copy` static blocks inside it — is hung off
  that event by `assets/wholesale-confirmation.js`. Because the event name is
  the app's and may change under us, the script also watches the app block: once
  the visitor has pressed the submit button and the app has removed it, the
  panel is shown as well. The panel is rendered hidden and stays hidden with
  JavaScript off, where the app's own message still appears.

  There is no login, gated catalog or portal behind this form. A submission is
  recorded by the app as a tagged customer and reviewed by Treat House staff by
  hand; what the applicant is told afterwards is copy, not theme logic.

  The hand-built version this replaces — fifteen fields on a native contact form,
  with the certificate as a mailto panel because that form carries no attachment —
  is in git immediately before this commit.
{% endcomment %}

<div
  id="apply"
  class="section section--page-width spacing-style color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}"
  style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: section.settings %}"
  data-testid="wholesale-form-{{ section.id }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div
    class="wholesale-form"
    style="--th-caret: url({{ 'icon-ui-caret-down.svg' | asset_url }});"
    data-wholesale-form
  >
    {% content_for 'blocks' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      The confirmation. Rendered on every load but hidden until the script sees
      the app accept a submission, so the merchant edits its words in the editor
      and nobody reaches it without sending anything. `tabindex="-1"` lets the
      script move focus to it when it appears, so a screen reader hears the news.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture sent -%}
      {% content_for 'block', type: '_ui-heading', id: 'sent-heading' %}
      {% content_for 'block', type: '_ui-text', id: 'sent-copy' %}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    <div
      class="wholesale-form__sent"
      data-wholesale-form-sent
      role="status"
      aria-live="polite"
      tabindex="-1"
      hidden
    >
      {% render 'ui-confirmation-panel', content: sent %}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<script src="{{ 'wholesale-confirmation.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

{% stylesheet %}
  /* The #apply anchor lands under the sticky header without this. */
  #apply {
    scroll-margin-block-start: calc(var(--header-height, 60px) + 16px);
  }

  /*
   * 630 centred, whatever the page is doing around it. The section already sits in
   * the `.section` grid's central column, so this narrows that column rather than
   * padding itself — see the page geometry note in CLAUDE.md.
   */
  .wholesale-form {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    inline-size: 100%;
    max-inline-size: 630px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /*
   * The heading and its line are drawn wider than the form — 716 against 630 — and
   * centred on it. They are blocks now rather than a wrapper of their own, because
   * the app's form is a block too and one `content_for` renders the lot; so the
   * width is given to the two that want it instead of to a box around them.
   */
  .wholesale-form > :is(.ui-heading, .ui-text) {
    inline-size: 100%;
    max-inline-size: 716px;
    margin: 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* The app's block, and the 48 the file leaves between the copy and the first
     field — 32 of it here, 16 from the column's gap. */
  .wholesale-form > .shopify-block {
    inline-size: 100%;
    margin-block-start: 32px;
  }

  /* The confirmation takes the form's place and its width. The heading and copy
     blocks inside it are centred by the panel, not by the rule above. */
  .wholesale-form__sent {
    inline-size: 100%;
    margin-block-start: 32px;
    outline: none;
  }

  .wholesale-form__sent[hidden],
  .wholesale-form > .shopify-block[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     The skin.

     Everything below dresses markup this theme does not write. Each rule is
     weighed at two classes so it clears the app's own single-class stylesheet,
     which is injected into the head at runtime and would otherwise win on order.

     Reached through `.wholesale-form` rather than through the app's own mount
     class, so the skin travels with the section and cannot leak into any other
     place a merchant drops the block.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

  /* The app draws its form as a grey card. The file draws no card — the fields sit
     on the page — so the box is taken off rather than restyled. */
  /* The app writes the card's background, width and height inline as well, hence
     the flags here — same reason as the fields below. The shadow is what draws the
     card once its background is gone. */
  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container,
  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container.boxed {
    inline-size: 100% !important;
    block-size: auto !important;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 0;
    background: none !important;
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  /*
   * The app's own title and strapline. The section already carries a heading and a
   * line of copy as blocks above, so this is the same thing said twice — hidden
   * rather than restyled. The rules under it stay: the app puts that header back
   * the moment the form is edited in its admin, and a merchant who wants it can
   * drop this one rule.
   */
  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-header {
    display: none;
  }

  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-header h2 {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family) !important;
    font-size: 24px;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary) !important;
  }

  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-header p {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body) !important;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray) !important;
  }

  /*
   * Two columns of 305 with 20 between, which is the 630 the form is, and 16 down
   * — the same figures the hand-built version used. The app lays its body out with
   * flex-wrap and its own half-width class, so the pair of numbers goes on the gap
   * and the half is recomputed against it.
   */
  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-body {
    gap: 16px 20px;
  }

  .wholesale-form .clay-half-width {
    flex: 0 0 calc(50% - 10px);
    max-inline-size: calc(50% - 10px);
  }

  .wholesale-form .clay-full-width {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    max-inline-size: 100%;
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    /* One column below 750, where the page has nothing like 630 to give it. */
    .wholesale-form .clay-half-width {
      flex-basis: 100%;
      max-inline-size: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* Label over field, 7px apart — [ui-input]'s own arrangement. */
  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-text-input-container {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 7px;
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-text-input-label {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent);
    font-weight: var(--font-accent--weight-semibold, 600);
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  /*
   * The field itself, declared through the same custom properties [ui-input] uses
   * rather than through its figures, so the app's fields move when the kit does.
   */
  /*
   * Three of these carry `!important`, and only these three. The app writes its
   * font and its background onto every field as an inline style, and its height
   * with an `!important` of its own — none of which a selector can outrank, however
   * it is weighed. Everything else here wins on specificity alone and is left
   * plain, so the flag stays a signal that something on the other side is fighting
   * rather than a habit.
   *
   * The values come from the same custom properties [ui-input] uses rather than
   * from its figures, so the app's fields move when the kit does.
   */
  .wholesale-form :is(
    .elements-preview-container-text-input,
    .phone-input-container input
  ) {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: var(--ui-input-height) !important;
    padding: var(--ui-input-padding);
    border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border-gray);
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white) !important;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body) !important;
    font-weight: var(--ui-font-body-weight);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    transition: border-color var(--animation-speed) ease;
  }

  .wholesale-form .elements-preview-container-text-input::placeholder,
  .wholesale-form .phone-input-container input::placeholder {
    color: var(--ui-color-gray);
  }

  /* The border stays engaged once the field holds a value, not only while focused —
     the same rule [ui-input] carries, and the design's focus indicator. */
  .wholesale-form :is(.elements-preview-container-text-input, .phone-input-container input):is(
    :hover,
    :focus,
    :not(:placeholder-shown)
  ) {
    border-color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  /*
   * The border turning purple is the design's focus indicator, and [ui-input] drops
   * the theme's generic ring for exactly that reason: it measures 6.4:1 between the
   * idle and the focused state, above the 3:1 WCAG 2.4.11 asks for, and a second
   * ring 3px outside it only doubles the mark. Kept as a transparent outline rather
   * than `none`, because forced-colours modes ignore borders and would otherwise
   * show nothing at all.
   */
  .wholesale-form :is(
    .elements-preview-container-text-input,
    .phone-input-container input
  ):is(:focus, :focus-visible) {
    outline: 2px solid transparent;
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /*
   * The dialling code, as [ui-phone-field] draws it (Figma 508:13455): a filled
   * purple square with the code in white and a caret beside it, not a white field
   * like the number next to it. The app renders a bare `<select>`, so the block is
   * the select itself — its chrome comes off and the fill goes on.
   *
   * The caret is a pseudo-element on the grid that holds the pair, because a
   * `<select>` can carry none of its own. It is the kit's own glyph, painted
   * through a mask so it takes a colour rather than carrying one; the URL arrives
   * as `--th-caret` on the section, since a stylesheet block is plain CSS
   * and cannot ask Liquid for an asset.
   */
  /*
   * The columns are named rather than left to the app, which sizes the first one to
   * whatever width the `<select>` came out at. Narrowing the select to the kit's 44
   * square did not narrow that column with it, so 15px of empty column sat between
   * the code and the number on top of the 7px gap.
   */
  .wholesale-form .phone-input-fields {
    position: relative;
    grid-template-columns: var(--ui-input-height, 44px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 7px;
  }

  .wholesale-form .country-code-select {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    inline-size: var(--ui-input-height, 44px) !important;
    block-size: var(--ui-input-height, 44px) !important;
    padding: 0 10px 0 0 !important;
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body) !important;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.35;
    border: none !important;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-primary) !important;
    /* The app draws its own caret as a data-URI background on the select, dark and
       sized 16px at the right edge — exactly where ours goes. Off, so the masked
       one below is the only one there. */
    background-image: none !important;
    color: var(--ui-color-white) !important;
    text-align: center;
    text-align-last: center;
    appearance: none;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* A dropped-open select paints its list with the page's colours, not the block's. */
  .wholesale-form .country-code-select > option {
    color: var(--ui-color-black);
  }

  /* White on the purple, and inside the block — the ring the kit gives this one. */
  .wholesale-form .country-code-select:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ui-color-white);
    outline-offset: -3px;
  }

  .wholesale-form .phone-input-fields::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    inset-inline-start: calc(var(--ui-input-height, 44px) - 16px);
    inline-size: 8px;
    block-size: 10px;
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white);
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    mask-image: var(--th-caret);
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-position: center;
    mask-size: contain;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /*
   * The certificate panel. The app already draws it dashed and 119 tall against the
   * 121 the file gives [ui-upload-field], so this only changes the corner and the
   * colour it is drawn in.
   */
  .wholesale-form .file-upload-container {
    border: 2px dashed var(--ui-color-border-gray);
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white);
  }

  /*
   * The control inside the panel. It is a `<label>` over the file input rather than
   * a button, and the app paints it black inline; this is the kit's secondary
   * button, which is what a control on a white panel wears.
   */
  .wholesale-form .browse-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    block-size: 40px;
    padding: 0 24px;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--ui-color-primary);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-white) !important;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent) !important;
    font-size: 12px !important;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color var(--animation-values), color var(--animation-values);
  }

  .wholesale-form .browse-button:hover {
    background-color: var(--ui-color-primary) !important;
    color: var(--ui-color-white);
  }

  /* The line inside the panel is a bare `<p>` the app paints inline, hence the
     flags — same reason as everywhere else in this skin. */
  .wholesale-form .file-upload-placeholder,
  .wholesale-form .file-upload-placeholder p {
    font-family: var(--ui-font-body) !important;
    font-size: 14px !important;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ui-color-gray) !important;
  }

  /*
   * The submit, in the primary button's clothes. Written out rather than rendered
   * through [ui-button] because the app draws its own element — and it arrives
   * carrying `.button.primary`, which Horizon's untouched `.button` also answers
   * to, so this has to outweigh that as well as the app's own rule.
   */
  /* The submit sits in a flex item that otherwise shrinks to the button. */
  .wholesale-form .submit-button-container {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  .wholesale-form button.button.primary {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 44px;
    margin-block-start: 32px;
    padding: 0 32px;
    border: none;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--ui-color-primary) !important;
    color: var(--ui-color-white);
    font-family: var(--ui-font-accent) !important;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.3;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color var(--animation-values), color var(--animation-values);
  }

  /*
   * Only while it can be pressed. The app disables the submit until the required
   * fields are filled and fades it to 0.7 inline; left unqualified, the hover
   * still fired and the button turned sky blue under the pointer — which is the
   * kit's "this is live" state on a control that is not.
   */
  .wholesale-form button.button.primary:not(:disabled):hover {
    background-color: var(--ui-color-sky-blue) !important;
    color: var(--ui-color-primary);
  }

  .wholesale-form button.button.primary:disabled {
    cursor: not-allowed !important;
  }

  /*
   * The app hangs a tooltip off the disabled submit — "Please fill out the
   * following required fields:" — from a zero-sized wrapper at the button's left
   * edge, so it opened outside the form and was cut off by the page. Hidden rather
   * than repositioned, by request.
   *
   * What it was carrying is not lost: the fields it names are the ones already
   * marked with a red asterisk, and the submit stays visibly disabled until they
   * are filled.
   */
  .wholesale-form .submit-button-container .tooltip {
    display: none;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Wholesale application",
  "tag": "section",
  "blocks": [{ "type": "_ui-heading" }, { "type": "_ui-text" }, { "type": "@app" }],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The form is the Clay B2B Wholesale app's registration block. Add it below, then pick which of the app's forms it shows. Fields, order and what is required are set in the app, not here. Submissions are recorded by the app as tagged customers and reviewed by hand; the thank-you panel shown after sending is the two static blocks named Sent heading and Sent copy."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:content.colors"
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "t:settings.color_scheme",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "t:content.padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "t:settings.top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 120,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 80
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "t:settings.bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 120,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 80
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Wholesale application",
      "category": "t:categories.forms",
      "blocks": {
        "heading": {
          "type": "_ui-heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<h2>Apply for <em>wholesale</em></h2>"
          }
        },
        "intro": {
          "type": "_ui-text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Fill in the form below and we will be in touch by email.</p>"
          }
        },
        "sent-heading": {
          "type": "_ui-heading",
          "static": true,
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<h2>Thanks, your application <em>is in</em></h2>",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "heading_size": 36,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28
          }
        },
        "sent-copy": {
          "type": "_ui-text",
          "static": true,
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A real person will review it and email you pricing and ordering details. Questions in the meantime? Write to info@treathouse.com.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["heading", "intro"]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
